      <Output>
        <StdOut>Set apart for WebKit: F-5: WebKit shades a pattern whose tile is a few pixels across darker than Chromium and with a grain, from the one tile the document gives, and otherwise as the pattern's phase falls on the pixels; and WebKit hints the glyphs of the faces the document embeds, as its Linux build does whatever fontconfig, text-rendering or its own settings say, while Chromium draws them unhinted. WebKit's image of teanwest has 133 pixels outside the tolerance of Chromium's (each color channel within the range the other image takes within 1 pixel, give or take 4 levels, or at an edge within 80 levels of it, and within 0.4 of the contrast around it, once both are blurred by 1 pixel), by up to 79 levels; see browsers/webkit/differences/teanwest.png.</StdOut>
